diff --git a/docs/sql-reference.md b/docs/sql-reference.md
index 983bb10..143c1a8 100644
--- a/docs/sql-reference.md
+++ b/docs/sql-reference.md
@@ -177,6 +177,78 @@ order.
 
 `DISTINCT` is accepted by every aggregate except `ANY_VALUE`.
 
+## Window functions
+
+```sql
+SELECT name,
+       ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY city ORDER BY signup_date) AS nth,
+       SUM(total)   OVER (PARTITION BY city)                      AS city_total
+FROM customers
+```
+
+An `OVER` clause turns a call into a window function: the value is computed
+over a set of rows instead of over one row, and the query keeps every input
+row. `PARTITION BY` splits the input the way `GROUP BY` does, including its
+rule that two nulls are the same key; without it the whole input is one
+partition. The `ORDER BY` inside `OVER` accepts the same direction and null
+placement syntax as the statement's own, and defaults null placement from
+`null_ordering` in the same way.
+
+`ROW_NUMBER`, `RANK` and `DENSE_RANK` take no arguments and are only usable
+with `OVER`. Rows whose ordering values are all equal are peers: `ROW_NUMBER`
+numbers them anyway, `RANK` gives each the position its group started at, and
+`DENSE_RANK` counts groups. Any aggregate may take an `OVER` clause too, in
+which case it folds the whole partition when there is no `ORDER BY`, and
+otherwise every row through the end of the current peer group.
+
+A `ROWS` frame narrows what an aggregate folds, counting rows rather than
+peers:
+
+```sql
+SUM(total) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS 6 PRECEDING)
+SUM(total) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)
+SUM(total) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING)
+```
+
+Both spellings mean the same thing, the frame always ends at the current row,
+and `UNBOUNDED PRECEDING` reaches back to the start of the partition. Because
+the frame counts rows, two rows with equal ordering values sit at different
+positions and read different windows, which is the opposite of the peer rule
+above. A frame needs an ordering to count along, and a ranking function cannot
+take one.
+
+A `RANGE` frame measures its reach in the ordering value instead of in rows:
+
+```sql
+SUM(total) OVER (ORDER BY day RANGE 6 PRECEDING)
+SUM(total) OVER (ORDER BY day RANGE BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)
+```
+
+Every row that sorts no later than the current one and whose `day` lies within
+6 of it is folded, and because peers sort together the frame runs through the
+current row's peers as well. The distance follows the ordering direction, so
+under `DESC` the frame reaches rows with larger values. A `RANGE` frame with a
+distance needs exactly one numeric ordering key, and a row whose ordering value
+is `NULL` has no distance to anything, so it folds its peers alone.
+`RANGE UNBOUNDED PRECEDING` is the default fold spelled out.
+
+A frame may end with an exclusion:
+
+```sql
+SUM(total) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS 6 PRECEDING EXCLUDE CURRENT ROW)
+SUM(total) OVER (ORDER BY day RANGE 6 PRECEDING EXCLUDE TIES)
+SUM(total) OVER (ORDER BY day RANGE UNBOUNDED PRECEDING EXCLUDE GROUP)
+```
+
+`EXCLUDE CURRENT ROW` drops the current row from its frame, `EXCLUDE TIES`
+drops the current row's peers but keeps the row itself, and `EXCLUDE GROUP`
+drops both. `EXCLUDE NO OTHERS` spells out the default and drops nothing. A
+frame emptied this way folds no rows at all, so `COUNT` reads 0 and every
+other aggregate reads `NULL`.
+
+`DISTINCT` cannot be combined with `OVER`, and a window may only appear in the
+select list and in `ORDER BY`, never in `WHERE`, `GROUP BY` or `HAVING`.
+
